 ## Overview
-The 3D Widget Interaction Model provides a framework for creating interactive graphical objects (widgets) that exist within a 3D scene and can be directly manipulated by the user. It solves the problem of coupling user input events (mouse, keyboard) to specific geometric representations in the rendering window, allowing for immediate visual feedback and data modification. Unlike standard interactor styles which manipulate the camera or view, this model associates observers with a `vtkRenderWindow` that maintain a scene representation, enabling direct manipulation of data structures such as streamlines or clipping volumes [S1, book].
+The 3D Widget Interaction Model provides a framework for creating interactive, scene-represented objects that users can directly manipulate to control visualization parameters or data processing pipelines. It solves the problem of bridging user input gestures with specific application logic by decoupling the interaction handling from the camera control. Unlike standard interactor styles which manipulate the view, this model associates a visual representation with the widget, allowing the widget's state to drive downstream data transformations (such as streamlines or clipping planes) via an event-driven observer pattern.
 
